From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Subject: power: supply: bq24190_charger: stop the i2c watchdog waking the system

bq24190_probe() calls enable_irq_wake() on the charger's interrupt
unconditionally, so on every board using this driver the charger is a system
wake source. bq24190_pm_suspend() then calls bq24190_register_reset(), which
returns every register to its default -- including the CTTC watchdog field,
re-arming the 40 s i2c watchdog that bq24190_set_config() deliberately turned
off at probe. Its comment explains why it is off:

	 * According to the "Host Mode and default Mode" section of the
	 * manual, a write to any register causes the bq24190 to switch
	 * from default mode to host mode.  It will switch back to default
	 * mode after a WDT timeout unless the WDT is turned off as well.

Nothing pets that watchdog while the system is asleep, so 40 s into the suspend
it expires, raises WATCHDOG_FAULT, pulses INT, and the machine wakes up. Then
bq24190_pm_resume() register-resets and re-runs set_config(), so by the time
anyone looks the watchdog reads 0 again and the wake has no visible cause.

On mt8173 (amazon-suez) this makes suspend useless whenever the tablet is
plugged in. Measured repeatedly with the SoC's own sleep counter, asking for a
100 s suspend:

	state write rc=0, uptime 831.78 -> 874.40
	woke by EINT, slept 1399426 ticks, raw_sta = 0x40      (= 42.7 s)

and snapshotting /proc/interrupts either side of the sleep shows exactly one
line moving:

	25:  +3   mt-eint  9  Edge  bq24190-charger

Note the charger is also the only permanently wake-armed interrupt on that
board -- every other wake source arms itself during suspend through the wakeirq
framework -- so it is the one that cannot be turned off from the outside.

Re-apply the configuration after the reset, exactly as bq24190_pm_resume()
already does. The watchdog goes back off, the chip keeps the settings it was
given for the duration of the sleep instead of spending it at reset defaults,
and genuine charger interrupts still wake the system, which is what the wake
irq is for.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq24190_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq24190_charger.c
@@ -2264,6 +2264,22 @@
 
 	bq24190_register_reset(bdi);
 
+	/*
+	 * The reset returned every register to its default, and that re-armed
+	 * the chip's 40 s i2c watchdog which bq24190_set_config() deliberately
+	 * turned off. Nothing pets it while the system is asleep, so it
+	 * expires, raises WATCHDOG_FAULT and pulses INT -- and probe() arms
+	 * that interrupt as a system wake source unconditionally, so the
+	 * machine wakes back up. Measured on mt8173 (amazon-suez) as a wake
+	 * every ~42 s for the whole of every suspend.
+	 *
+	 * So re-apply the configuration: the watchdog goes back off and the
+	 * chip keeps the settings it was given for the duration of the sleep.
+	 * Genuine charger events still wake the system, which is what the wake
+	 * irq is for.
+	 */
+	bq24190_set_config(bdi);
+
 	if (error >= 0) {
 		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(bdi->dev);
 	}
